@episode 12
@title The Demonstration

@chunk 01
This is Catastrophes. Book Two, Episode Twelve: The Demonstration.

When we left the Pacific, the United States had spent three years discovering that winning the Battle of Hawaii was not the same thing as winning the war.

Japan’s opening plan had depended on surprise. The American Pacific Fleet was supposed to be sitting at Pearl Harbor when the first Japanese aircraft arrived. Instead, because the fleet was already at sea on active exercises, what Japanese planners intended as a raid became a fleet battle against an alerted enemy.

The Americans won.

Strategically, anyway.

They lost ships. They lost aircraft. They lost men. But the fleet survived, and Japan lost something much harder to replace than steel: experienced carrier pilots and the assumption that the opening months of the war would belong entirely to Japan.

The phrase that entered American memory was simple.

They tried to catch us asleep.

We were ready.

That was nineteen forty-one.

By the summer of nineteen forty-four, Germany was gone.

The Western Allies had landed in France the previous September. German forces had fought hard, but the combination of Allied material superiority, Soviet pressure from the east, and growing fracture inside the German state had made the outcome increasingly obvious by spring. In July, senior officers and civilian officials moved against the Nazi leadership. Parts of the regime collapsed. Other parts attempted to keep fighting. For several extremely confusing weeks, nobody was entirely certain who was authorized to surrender what.

By August, organized German resistance had ended.

The Fascist War continued in the Pacific.

Japan was losing.

Its merchant fleet was shrinking. Fuel shortages were severe. American submarines were making transport increasingly dangerous. Allied aircraft could reach more of the Japanese home islands. The Japanese navy could still inflict damage, but it could no longer reliably contest control of the western Pacific.

And then, in New Mexico, the United States built something new.

Not a larger bomber.

Not a better submarine.

Not another carrier.

A weapon that made every previous calculation about industrial warfare look temporary.

The first atomic device was successfully tested in July nineteen forty-four.

President Roosevelt received confirmation in Washington.

He read the message twice.

Then he asked a question that would dominate the rest of the war.

Who do we use it on?

@chunk 02
The Manhattan Project had begun from fear.

That point is sometimes lost because later generations know how the project ended.

In nineteen forty-two, nobody knew whether Germany was building an atomic weapon.

Scientists knew nuclear fission could release extraordinary amounts of energy. They knew German physicists understood the same physics. They knew several of the most important European nuclear scientists had fled fascism. They also knew that a state willing to build concentration camps and wage continental war might be willing to use almost any weapon it could produce.

So the United States built laboratories.

It built reactors.

It built enormous industrial facilities whose purpose was concealed even from many of the people working inside them.

It consumed quantities of copper, silver, steel, electricity, and money that would have caused a congressional investigation under almost any other circumstances.

And for much of the project, the question was not whether the United States should possess an atomic bomb.

The question was whether Germany would possess one first.

Then Germany collapsed.

The bomb did not.

This created a problem.

A project built to answer one strategic question had survived long enough to encounter another.

Japan was still fighting.

American planners were preparing for the possibility of invasion.

Casualty estimates varied wildly because casualty estimates always vary wildly when they are trying to describe a battle that has not happened yet.

Some estimates were relatively modest.

Some were catastrophic.

Japanese preparations for homeland defense suggested that an invasion would be expensive even if final defeat was inevitable.

The atomic weapon offered another possibility.

Use it.

The first target studies were exactly what you would expect.

Industrial centers.

Military facilities.

Ports.

Cities associated with war production.

The argument was straightforward.

A weapon powerful enough to destroy a major target in a single attack might shock the Japanese government into surrender before invasion became necessary.

There was another argument.

Do not use it on people at all.

This was not initially the dominant position.

It was not even a single position.

Scientists, military officers, diplomats, and civilian officials reached it for different reasons.

Some believed killing tens of thousands of civilians without warning would be morally indefensible.

Some worried that if the first atomic bomb was used on a city, every future atomic weapon would begin from that precedent.

Some doubted one destroyed city would force surrender anyway.

Some wanted the Soviet Union and other governments to understand what the weapon could do before anyone had to decide whether to use another one.

And some made an argument that sounded very familiar in nineteen forty-four.

Once you take the first irreversible step, what determines the last one?

@chunk 03
The demonstration proposal took shape during August.

The idea was not complicated.

Select an uninhabited target.

Invite observers.

Tell the Japanese government exactly where and when the weapon would be used.

Then destroy something large enough that nobody could mistake what had happened.

There were practical objections.

The United States had very few atomic devices.

If the bomb failed in front of international observers, the psychological effect could be disastrous.

If Japan simply ignored the demonstration, the Allies would have spent a weapon without ending the war.

Military planners also pointed out that a demonstration in an empty desert did not prove the weapon could destroy a defended industrial city.

Scientists answered that physics did not become less convincing because the target was unoccupied.

The most serious objection came from uncertainty.

Nobody knew what Japan would do.

That uncertainty is why the cabinet discussions matter.

Roosevelt had spent most of his presidency surrounded by arguments about preventive action. Sometimes government needed to act before danger became catastrophe. Sometimes acting early created the catastrophe.

Atomic weapons made that problem immediate.

At one meeting, Secretary of War Henry Stimson argued that the United States should make the nature of the weapon unmistakable before using it against a populated target.

General Leslie Groves objected that the weapon had been built for war, not theater.

Scientists from the project circulated memoranda warning that the first use would establish the political meaning of atomic weapons for generations.

A young War Department logistics officer named Margaret Ward appears in the distribution list of one of those memoranda. There is no evidence she had any influence on the decision. At this point, she was simply one of several junior officials whose job required them to think about what happened after decisions were made.

The Joint Chiefs wanted maximum pressure on Japan and minimum delay.

The State Department wanted Soviet observers present.

The Navy wanted everyone to stop assuming Japan would surrender because of one explosion.

Roosevelt listened.

And eventually he decided on a demonstration.

Not because he had become certain it would work.

Because uncertainty cut both ways.

If the demonstration failed, the United States could still reconsider.

If the first bomb destroyed a city, nobody could reconsider that.

This distinction became famous later.

At the time, it was simply a decision made by a tired president in the fourth year of a war.

@chunk 04
The location chosen was a dry basin in the southwestern United States.

Far from major population centers.

Large enough to control access.

Close enough to existing military infrastructure to support the operation.

The target was deliberately artificial.

Engineers constructed a group of reinforced structures around a central tower.

There were concrete walls.

Steel frames.

Rail cars.

Fuel tanks.

Aircraft fuselage sections.

Telephone poles.

Sections of bridgework.

Vehicles.

Instrument shelters.

The point was partly scientific.

Different materials would show different effects at different distances.

It was also political.

The demonstration needed to look like something.

An explosion in empty desert would be enormous, but scale is difficult to understand without reference.

A flattened test village was harder to misunderstand.

The Japanese government was informed through neutral intermediaries that the United States possessed a new weapon of extraordinary destructive power.

The message gave a date.

A location.

And a warning.

Send observers if you wish.

The Soviet Union was invited.

Britain was present.

Representatives from several neutral governments were included.

Japanese officials did not attend directly.

Two representatives from the Swiss mission carried instructions to observe on Japan’s behalf.

This would later produce decades of argument over whether they were truly authorized to negotiate or merely report.

At five thirty in the morning on September second, nineteen forty-four, the device detonated.

People tens of miles away saw the flash through protective glass.

The tower disappeared.

The artificial structures near it disappeared with it.

Steel frames twisted.

Concrete shattered.

Rail cars overturned.

The desert surface fused.

The shock wave arrived after the light.

That delay appears in almost every account.

People saw something impossible.

Then they waited.

Then the air struck them.

One British observer wrote that the most frightening part was not the explosion.

It was the pause.

For several seconds, the world had changed and nobody had heard it yet.

@chunk 05
Film of the demonstration was released within hours.

This was intentional.

The United States wanted Japan to understand what had happened.

It also wanted everyone else to understand.

Newsreels showed the flash.

The rising column.

The expanding cloud.

Then the camera moved across the remains of the target structures.

The images were repeated in theaters around the world.

For the first time, ordinary people saw an atomic explosion.

Nobody had died.

That fact mattered.

It did not make the weapon comforting.

It made it strange.

The first public image of atomic war was not bodies.

It was absence.

A tower was there.

Then it was not.

Buildings had existed.

Then they were scattered across the desert.

Newspapers struggled for vocabulary.

Sun.

Star.

Artificial lightning.

A second dawn.

None of these descriptions were especially good.

They survived anyway.

Roosevelt addressed the country that evening.

He explained that the United States had developed a weapon based on the release of atomic energy.

He said the demonstration had been conducted where no civilian population would be endangered.

He called on Japan to surrender.

And then he said something carefully vague.

The United States was capable of repeating the demonstration under different circumstances.

Everyone understood what that meant.

The Japanese government did not surrender.

Not immediately.

This is the part of the story that later versions sometimes compress.

The bomb exploded on September second.

The war did not end on September third.

Inside the Japanese government, the demonstration intensified an argument already underway.

Some officials believed further resistance had become irrational.

Others argued that the United States might possess only one weapon.

Some believed surrender without guarantees for the Emperor could produce revolution or military revolt.

Army hardliners insisted that invasion could still impose enough casualties to improve surrender terms.

The Navy was less optimistic.

American intelligence intercepted enough of this debate to understand that the demonstration had changed the argument.

It had not settled it.

So the United States waited.

For a little while.

@chunk 06
The Soviet Union complicated everything.

Relations between Washington and Moscow were still cooperative, but nobody confused cooperation with trust.

The Soviet government had been informed about the atomic project in deliberately incomplete terms. Soviet intelligence knew more than American officials realized.

After the demonstration, there was no longer much point pretending the weapon did not exist.

Stalin wanted technical access.

Roosevelt refused.

Stalin wanted assurances that atomic weapons would not be used to dictate the postwar settlement.

Roosevelt offered no such assurance.

At the same time, Soviet forces were preparing to enter the war against Japan.

Japanese diplomats knew this.

They had spent months hoping Moscow might mediate some form of negotiated peace.

The atomic demonstration badly damaged that strategy.

Soviet entry could destroy it completely.

The Japanese cabinet therefore faced several pressures at once.

The navy was weakening.

The blockade was tightening.

American conventional bombing was expanding.

An invasion looked possible.

The Soviet Union might enter the war.

And the United States had just publicly demonstrated a weapon capable of destroying an entire military complex in seconds.

The decisive question became the Emperor.

American policy had been ambiguous about the imperial institution.

Some officials wanted unconditional surrender interpreted literally.

Others believed removing the Emperor was unnecessary and might make surrender harder.

After the demonstration, Roosevelt authorized a clarification.

Japan would be required to surrender its armed forces and accept occupation.

War crimes would be prosecuted.

Japanese military power would be dismantled.

But the Emperor could remain, subject to the authority of the occupation.

This was not presented as a concession.

It was presented as an explanation.

The distinction was useful for everyone.

The Japanese government could say it had preserved the throne.

The United States could say it had not negotiated away the surrender.

And people who dislike diplomatic ambiguity have been arguing about it ever since.

It was an early example of a settlement that worked partly because everyone was allowed to describe it differently.

That would become a very important diplomatic skill, one that future generations would refine and use again.

@chunk 07
Japan surrendered in December.

There was no single moment when every argument ended.

There rarely is.

The Soviet threat to enter the war destroyed the last serious hope that Moscow might mediate a negotiated settlement.

The atomic demonstration convinced much of the civilian leadership that continued resistance risked destruction on a scale they could not control.

The future of the Emperor mattered.

The blockade mattered.

Conventional bombing mattered.

And the possibility of Soviet entry mattered enormously.

The Emperor intervened.

Army officers attempted to prevent the surrender announcement from being broadcast.

They failed.

On December eleventh, nineteen forty-four, the imperial rescript was transmitted.

The Fascist War was over.

The atomic weapon had never been used against a human target.

This became one of the foundational facts of postwar politics.

It also created a historical temptation.

Because the demonstration was followed by surrender, later generations often treated the demonstration as proof that atomic restraint worked.

The evidence is not that clean.

Japan was already losing.

The blockade mattered.

Conventional bombing mattered.

Soviet entry mattered.

The future of the Emperor mattered.

Internal Japanese politics mattered.

The atomic demonstration mattered.

History is allowed to have more than one cause.

What changed after the war was not that everyone agreed the bomb had forced surrender.

What changed was that everyone had seen the bomb.

And nobody had yet crossed the line of using one on a city.

That created a political fact more important than the argument over exactly why Japan surrendered.

There was still a first use to prevent.

Every government understood that.

Every scientist understood that.

Every military staff understood that.

The United States possessed atomic weapons.

Other countries would eventually learn how to build them.

And the next war might not provide enough time for another philosophical discussion in a conference room.

The problem was no longer how to end the Fascist War.

The problem was what happened when two governments had the bomb.

@chunk 08
The immediate postwar celebrations were enormous.

So was the relief.

The Great War had ended after four years.

The Fascist War had ended after five.

Germany was defeated.

Japan was occupied.

The United States had emerged with extraordinary industrial power.

The Soviet Union had paid an almost unimaginable human cost and now dominated much of eastern Europe.

Britain and France were victorious and exhausted.

China had survived years of invasion and internal conflict.

And sitting underneath all of this was the atomic weapon.

For several months, Americans tended to talk about the demonstration as another example of national preparation.

Scientists had warned of a possible German bomb.

The government had acted before Germany could build one.

The project had succeeded.

The weapon had helped end the war without being used on a city.

To many Progressives, this looked like the strongest possible confirmation of everything they had argued since Columbia.

See the danger early.

Build the capacity before you need it.

Do not wait for catastrophe.

But atomic weapons contained a problem that lifeboats did not.

A lifeboat did not become more dangerous because another country also owned one.

An atomic bomb did.

If the United States kept atomic weapons because another power might eventually build them, then the other power had the same reason.

If the Soviet Union built one because the United States already had one, the United States had a reason to build more.

Each decision could be defensive.

Each decision could be rational.

And the result could be a system in which everyone became less safe.

People had seen this structure before.

That did not mean they knew how to solve it.

For the moment, the United States had the bomb.

The Soviet Union did not.

Stalin wanted one.

American officials wanted to preserve their advantage.

Scientists were already warning that the advantage would not last.

And somewhere inside the postwar bureaucracy, a small group of lawyers, diplomats, engineers, and military officers began asking a question that would define the next decade.

What if nobody gets the bomb?

Not metaphorically.

Not eventually.

Literally.

Nobody.

That is where we are going next.

Book Three.

The Atomic Settlement.

Thanks for listening.
